con is separately linked by the author site at https://zhanxy.xyz/Magpie-website/magpie-mark.png.","Source reviewed","2026-10-19","verified","section","metadata","Year","2026","year","list","Tracks","ordered",false,[78,79],"dimensions","3D","World models","Development stages",[83,84,79],"stages","Gameplay & code","Engines & scenes","Topics",[88,89,90,91,92],"tags","hybrid-renderer","executable-state","game-engine","render-server","research","Further reading",[95],{"_52":96,"_25":97},"Paper v1 latency and limitations","https://arxiv.org/html/2608.27168v1","Project source",[100],{"_52":98,"_25":101},"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.27168","Why it belongs here","Designers define scenes and rules in a conventional game engine. At runtime, the engine processes player actions and maintains state, while a rendering service transforms engine-produced whitebox frames into final visuals. It demonstrates a division between generative rendering and reproducible gameplay, primarily reducing prototype dependence on complete art assets. The paper reports chunk-based interaction delay and remaining visual-consistency limitations; it does not establish a production-ready game renderer.","detail","guidance","Resource type","Paper","Category","category","lead","summary","name","world","paper",[88,89,90,91,92],[122,123,113],[121,113],2026,[120],{"_52":96,"_25":97},"3d","code","scene",{"_125":126},"documents",{"_127":128,"_129":130},"en",[132,136,137,138,135,139,140,141,142,143,144,145,146,147,148,149,150,151,152,153,154,155,156,157,158,159,160,161,162,163,164,165,166,167,168,169,170,134,171,133,172,173,174,175,176,177,178,179,180,181,182,183,184,185,186,187,188,189,190,191,192],"zh",[131,132,133,134,135],"reading-guide","practical-guide","level-design-skill","deployment","publishing-platforms","tutorials-and-demos","coding-agent-game-code","game-testing-library","agents-notes","engines-notes","game-ai-notes","cadle-repositories","world-model-notes","research-index","production-research-note